1	// In-process, time bounded lease registry with append-only log persistence
2	// and crash-safe snapshots. Time only moves when the injected clock moves;
3	// nothing expires on its own. Tokens are derived from the injected clock as
4	// well, never from wall time.
5	//
6	// Resources may be declared with a share capacity: several holders then
7	// occupy the same resource at once, each holding one credential per share
8	// with its own independent expiry. An undeclared resource stays a
9	// single-occupancy resource exactly as before. Hierarchical quotas sit above
10	// resources: a parent quota bounds the sum of the shares occupied under every
11	// quota below it (and directly declared resources), and every level is
12	// enforced at once.
13	//
14	// Every resource keeps a credential epoch, starting at 1 on its first grant
15	// and advanced by exactly one per committed credential (retake, same-holder
16	// reacquisition, woken grant and holder transfer included). Renewals never
17	// advance it, nor do failed, queued, timed-out, cancelled or partially
18	// committed requests; it never moves backwards and survives restarts and
19	// compactions. The newest
20	// credential of an exclusive resource is therefore the leader epoch, and
21	// assertLease is the fence a holder passes only while it still is that
22	// leader.
23	//
24	// On top of that fence sit chain checkpoints: the current leader of an
25	// exclusive resource may atomically compare-and-commit a { height, hash }
26	// checkpoint by naming the version it last observed. The version starts at
27	// 1 and advances by exactly one per committed write, surviving lease
28	// turnover, restarts and compactions, so a stale holder that lost the lease
29	// can never overwrite the progress a successor recorded; a chain
30	// reorganization may lower the height freely. Checkpoints move no lease,
31	// epoch, counter or wait-queue state.
32	//
33	// Persistence layout:
34	//   <logPath>           append-only event log, one JSON object per line
35	//   <logPath>.snapshot  last compacted state image (single JSON line)
36	// Startup loads the snapshot (if any) and replays only the events past the
37	// compacted boundary marker. Compaction publishes both files through a
38	// temp-file + fsync + rename sequence, so a failure at any step leaves the
39	// previous snapshot and the previous log usable. Every event carries a
40	// monotonic sequence number; a snapshot found next to an un-exchanged
41	// original log (the log swap died after the snapshot rename) simply skips
42	// the already-folded events by their sequence ids, so each event applies
43	// exactly once regardless of which rename made it to disk.
